# How to cite this game, for anybody who writes about it — a talk, a paper on
# making software for children, a list of Godot projects. GitHub reads this file
# and offers the citation on the repository page.
cff-version: 1.2.0
# The title carries what the thing is, not only what it is called. A citation
# that reads "Organokov, M. (2026). Aava" tells a reader nothing they can act
# on; the archive, the citation box on GitHub and the BibTeX in the README all
# take this line, so it is the one place worth spending words.
title: "Aava: an open world that comes alive as you build in it"
message: "If you write about this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Mukharbek
    family-names: Organokov
    email: mukharbek.organokov@gmail.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-3093-3456"
abstract: >-
  Aava is a 3D exploration and building game for children, written in GDScript
  on Godot 4. The valley is a pure function of one integer seed: the ground,
  the river, the forest and the mountains are computed rather than stored, so
  nothing about the world has to be saved, sent between two machines playing
  together, or loaded from a file. What is saved is only what a child has
  changed.


  The loop is creation rather than collection. Everything built makes the
  valley more alive, and the more alive it is the more it gives back: plant a
  grove and birds arrive, feed an animal and it becomes a friend, raise a house
  with a bed and a fire and it becomes somewhere to spend the night. Coins come
  from looking after things, and are spent on tools that widen what a child can
  reach — an axe, shears, a lantern, a bicycle, a quad — rather than on numbers
  going up.


  It is played on a phone by two brothers, aged ten and six, and nearly every
  change in its history began with one of them saying what looked wrong. The
  test suite is written the same way round: each check corresponds to a bug
  that actually happened, and says in its own words what the wrong version
  looked like, so a regression is recognised rather than merely detected.


  The code is AGPL-3.0-or-later. The name Aava, the application id and the icon
  are reserved and are not covered by that licence; see NOTICE.md.
keywords:
  - game
  - godot
  - gdscript
  - children
  - procedural-generation
  - android
license: AGPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: "https://github.com/kabartay/aava"
version: 0.4.2
date-released: "2026-09-26"
